HYPOTHESIS (answer to the question): SAMPLE_RACE — i.e. the respondent's race (Black / Hispanic versus
White) — is the variable whose effect on BA degree completion decreases from 0.5024 to 0.0923 and becomes
insignificant when both SES and academic characteristics are included: a positive effect of being White
(equivalently a negative effect of being Black or Hispanic) on the odds of completing a BA is large and
highly significant on its own (White vs non-White log-odds +0.9735, OR 2.65, 95% CI [1.92, 3.65], p=3e-9;
Black vs White -0.8757, OR 0.42, p=2e-5; Hispanic vs White -0.7222, OR 0.49, p=0.003), but it collapses to
a small, non-significant residual once SES (0.4729 -> -0.2171 in the SES reverse check, still p<0.05) and
the academic block (ASVAB ability composite + class percentile, LR chi2(2)=217.6, p<1e-47) are jointly in
the model (White gap +0.0741, OR 1.08, 95% CI [0.76, 1.54], p=0.68, 92.4% attenuation; Black +0.1361,
p=0.54, 84.5% attenuation; reference-free 2-df race block LR chi2 drops 41.44, p=1e-9 -> 1.54, p=0.46).

WHAT THE COLLAPSE IS ATTRIBUTABLE TO: the academic block, not SES, and not merely their joint inclusion.
Entering academic characteristics alone already removes the race gap (Black -0.8757 -> +0.1481, p=0.50,
83% attenuation; 2-df race block p=0.60), whereas adding SES alone leaves the race effect large and highly
significant (Black -0.7497, p=0.0003; 2-df block p=2.8e-06, only 14% attenuation). Within the academic
block the ASVAB ability composite is the active mediator (race 2-df block p=0.37 with ABILITY entered but
p=0.002 with class PERCENTILE entered). Both blocks together give the fully adjusted, non-significant
coefficient quoted in the question. Interpretation: the racial gap in BA completion is statistically
explained away by prior academic resources/achievement rather than by family SES.

RULED OUT: FAMILY SIZE OF SAMPLE is not the culprit even though its unadjusted log-odds (-0.5077) happens
to match 0.5024 in magnitude — it is completely unaffected by the adjustment (-0.5085, p<1e-25, 0%
attenuation), and SES is not the culprit either (reverse check stays significant, and the question treats
SES as an included control). SAMPLE_SEX never reaches significance and does not attenuate (+0.2037,
p=0.125 -> +0.2412, p=0.093; 1-df block p=0.124 -> p=0.092).

CAVEATS CARRIED INTO THE ANSWER: the answer rests on the shrinkage pattern, not on exact reproduction —
no combination of focal variable, nested specification, model family (logit/probit/LPM) or scaling
(raw/standardised) out of ~1,000 tried reproduces 0.5024 and 0.0923 exactly (best absolute error 0.038,
obtained for a standardised-predictor logit of the White/non-White race contrast, 0.4752 -> 0.0362);
BA prevalence in the supplied file is only 2.69% (236 events, of which just 28 Black and 19 Hispanic BA
completers), so race-cell standard errors are wide, but a hand-rolled Firth penalised logistic on the fully
adjusted model reproduces the MLE coefficients to <1e-6 (no separation) and SEs rise only 7-10% from the
unadjusted to the fully adjusted model while the race coefficients move by 0.9-1.3 log-odds — so the loss
of significance is a genuine adjustment change, not an artefact of event sparsity. Collinearity is not the
driver either (max VIF 2.73 for the ability composite; entering the two academic variables one at a time
reproduces the collapse). Curriculum and the four ASVAB components described in datasets.json are absent
from the supplied CSV, so "academic characteristics" was operationalised as z(ABILITY: COMPOSITE OF ASVAB
SCORE) + z(PERCENTILE IN CLASS); that substitution is the main reason the quoted values are not reproduced
exactly. Finally, these are cross-sectional observational data: the reported collapse is statistical
adjustment (mediation-like attenuation), not an identified causal effect.
